{"path":"std/boxed/index.html","title":"Module boxed","description":"The Box<T> type for heap allocation.","body":{"type":"document","children":[{"type":"anchor","id":"main-content"},{"type":"heading","level":1,"id":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Module boxed"}]},{"type":"text","value":"1.0.0 · "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/src/alloc/lib.rs.html#225","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" type for heap allocation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/boxed/struct.Box.html","title":"struct std::boxed::Box","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"}]},{"type":"text","value":", casually referred to as a ‘box’, provides the simplest form of heap allocation in Rust. Boxes provide ownership for this allocation, and drop their contents when they go out of scope. Boxes also ensure that they never allocate more than "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"isize::MAX"},{"type":"text","value":" bytes."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Move a value from the stack to the heap by creating a "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/boxed/struct.Box.html","title":"struct std::boxed::Box","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code_block","language":null,"code":"let val: u8 = 5;\nlet boxed: Box<u8> = Box::new(val);"},{"type":"link","href":"https://play.rust-lang.org/?code=%23!%5Ballow(unused)%5D%0Afn+main()+%7B%0A++++let+val:+u8+=+5;%0A++++let+boxed:+Box%3Cu8%3E+=+Box::new(val);%0A%7D&edition=2024","title":"Run code","children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Move a value from a "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/boxed/struct.Box.html","title":"struct std::boxed::Box","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box"}]},{"type":"text","value":" back to the stack by "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/ops/trait.Deref.html","title":"trait std::ops::Deref","children":[{"type":"text","value":"dereferencing"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code_block","language":null,"code":"let boxed: Box<u8> = Box::new(5);\nlet val: u8 = *boxed;"},{"type":"link","href":"https://play.rust-lang.org/?code=%23!%5Ballow(unused)%5D%0Afn+main()+%7B%0A++++let+boxed:+Box%3Cu8%3E+=+Box::new(5);%0A++++let+val:+u8+=+*boxed;%0A%7D&edition=2024","title":"Run code","children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating a recursive data structure:"}]},{"type":"code_block","language":null,"code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum List<T> {\n    Cons(T, Box<List<T>>),\n    Nil,\n}\n\nlet list: List<i32> = List::Cons(1, Box::new(List::Cons(2, Box::new(List::Nil))));\nprintln!(\"{list:?}\");"},{"type":"link","href":"https://play.rust-lang.org/?code=%23!%5Ballow(unused)%5D%0Afn+main()+%7B%0A++++%23%5Ballow(dead_code)%5D%0A++++%23%5Bderive(Debug)%5D%0A++++enum+List%3CT%3E+%7B%0A++++++++Cons(T,+Box%3CList%3CT%3E%3E),%0A++++++++Nil,%0A++++%7D%0A++++%0A++++let+list:+List%3Ci32%3E+=+List::Cons(1,+Box::new(List::Cons(2,+Box::new(List::Nil))));%0A++++println!(%22%7Blist:?%7D%22);%0A%7D&edition=2024","title":"Run code","children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This will print "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Cons(1, Cons(2, Nil))"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recursive structures must be boxed, because if the definition of "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Cons"},{"type":"text","value":" looked like this:"}]},{"type":"link","href":"std/boxed/index.html#","title":"This example deliberately fails to compile","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ⓘ"}]},{"type":"code_block","language":null,"code":"Cons(T, List<T>),"},{"type":"link","href":"https://play.rust-lang.org/?code=%23!%5Ballow(unused)%5D%0Afn+main()+%7B%0A++++enum+List%3CT%3E+%7B%0A++++Cons(T,+List%3CT%3E),%0A++++%7D%0A%7D&edition=2024","title":"Run code","children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It wouldn’t work. This is because the size of a "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"List"},{"type":"text","value":" depends on how many elements are in the list, and so we don’t know how much memory to allocate for a "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Cons"},{"type":"text","value":". By introducing a "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/boxed/struct.Box.html","title":"struct std::boxed::Box","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"}]},{"type":"text","value":", which has a defined size, we know how big "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Cons"},{"type":"text","value":" needs to be."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"memory-layout","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Memory layout"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For non-zero-sized values, a "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/boxed/struct.Box.html","title":"struct std::boxed::Box","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box"}]},{"type":"text","value":" will use the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/alloc/struct.Global.html","title":"struct std::alloc::Global","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"Global"}]},{"type":"text","value":" allocator for its allocation. It is valid to convert both ways between a "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/boxed/struct.Box.html","title":"struct std::boxed::Box","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and a raw pointer allocated with the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/alloc/struct.Global.html","title":"struct std::alloc::Global","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"Global"}]},{"type":"text","value":" allocator, given that the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/alloc/struct.Layout.html","title":"struct std::alloc::Layout","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"Layout"}]},{"type":"text","value":" used with the allocator is correct for the type and the raw pointer points to a valid value of the right type. More precisely, a "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"value: *mut T"},{"type":"text","value":" that has been allocated with the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/alloc/struct.Global.html","title":"struct std::alloc::Global","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"Global"}]},{"type":"text","value":" allocator with "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Layout::for_value(&*value)"},{"type":"text","value":" may be converted into a box using "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/boxed/struct.Box.html#method.from_raw","title":"associated function std::boxed::Box::from_raw","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box::<T>::from_raw(value)"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Conversely, the memory backing a "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"value: *mut T"},{"type":"text","value":" obtained from "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/boxed/struct.Box.html#method.into_raw","title":"associated function std::boxed::Box::into_raw","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box::<T>::into_raw"}]},{"type":"text","value":" may be deallocated using the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/alloc/struct.Global.html","title":"struct std::alloc::Global","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"Global"}]},{"type":"text","value":" allocator with "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/alloc/struct.Layout.html#method.for_value","title":"associated function std::alloc::Layout::for_value","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"Layout::for_value(&*value)"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For zero-sized values, the "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box"},{"type":"text","value":" pointer has to be non-null and sufficiently aligned. The recommended way to build a Box to a ZST if "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box::new"},{"type":"text","value":" cannot be used is to use "},{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/ptr/struct.NonNull.html#method.dangling","title":"associated function std::ptr::NonNull::dangling","children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"ptr::NonNull::dangling"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On top of these basic layout requirements, a "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" must point to a valid value of "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"T"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So long as "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"T: Sized"},{"type":"text","value":", a "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" is guaranteed to be represented as a single pointer and is also ABI-compatible with C pointers (i.e. the C type "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"T*"},{"type":"text","value":"). This means that if you have extern “C” Rust functions that will be called from C, you can define those Rust functions using "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" types, and use "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"T*"},{"type":"text","value":" as corresponding type on the C side. As an example, consider this C header which declares functions that create and destroy some kind of "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Foo"},{"type":"text","value":" value:"}]},{"type":"code_block","language":"c","code":"/* C header */\n\n/* Returns ownership to the caller */\nstruct Foo* foo_new(void);\n\n/* Takes ownership from the caller; no-op when invoked with null */\nvoid foo_delete(struct Foo*);"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These two functions might be implemented in Rust as follows. Here, the "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"struct Foo*"},{"type":"text","value":" type from C is translated to "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<Foo>"},{"type":"text","value":", which captures the ownership constraints. Note also that the nullable argument to "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"foo_delete"},{"type":"text","value":" is represented in Rust as "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Option<Box<Foo>>"},{"type":"text","value":", since "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<Foo>"},{"type":"text","value":" cannot be null."}]},{"type":"code_block","language":null,"code":"#[repr(C)]\npub struct Foo;\n\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub extern \"C\" fn foo_new() -> Box<Foo> {\n    Box::new(Foo)\n}\n\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub extern \"C\" fn foo_delete(_: Option<Box<Foo>>) {}"},{"type":"link","href":"https://play.rust-lang.org/?code=%23!%5Ballow(unused)%5D%0Afn+main()+%7B%0A++++%23%5Brepr(C)%5D%0A++++pub+struct+Foo;%0A++++%0A++++%23%5Bunsafe(no_mangle)%5D%0A++++pub+extern+%22C%22+fn+foo_new()+-%3E+Box%3CFoo%3E+%7B%0A++++++++Box::new(Foo)%0A++++%7D%0A++++%0A++++%23%5Bunsafe(no_mangle)%5D%0A++++pub+extern+%22C%22+fn+foo_delete(_:+Option%3CBox%3CFoo%3E%3E)+%7B%7D%0A%7D&edition=2024","title":"Run code","children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Even though "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" has the same representation and C ABI as a C pointer, this does not mean that you can convert an arbitrary "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"T*"},{"type":"text","value":" into a "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" and expect things to work. "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" values will always be fully aligned, non-null pointers. Moreover, the destructor for "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" will attempt to free the value with the global allocator. In general, the best practice is to only use "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" for pointers that originated from the global allocator."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Important."}]},{"type":"text","value":" At least at present, you should avoid using "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" types for functions that are defined in C but invoked from Rust. In those cases, you should directly mirror the C types as closely as possible. Using types like "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" where the C definition is just using "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"T*"},{"type":"text","value":" can lead to undefined behavior, as described in "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/rust-lang/unsafe-code-guidelines/issues/198","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"rust-lang/unsafe-code-guidelines#198"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"considerations-for-unsafe-code","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Considerations for unsafe code"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Warning: This section is not normative and is subject to change, possibly being relaxed in the future! It is a simplified summary of the rules currently implemented in the compiler."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The aliasing rules for "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" are the same as for "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"&mut T"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" asserts uniqueness over its content. Using raw pointers derived from a box after that box has been mutated through, moved or borrowed as "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"&mut T"},{"type":"text","value":" is not allowed. For more guidance on working with box from unsafe code, see "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/rust-lang/unsafe-code-guidelines/issues/326","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"rust-lang/unsafe-code-guidelines#326"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"editions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Editions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A special case exists for the implementation of "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"IntoIterator"},{"type":"text","value":" for arrays on the Rust 2021 edition, as documented "},{"type":"link","href":"std/primitive.array.html","title":"primitive array","children":[{"type":"text","value":"here"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Unfortunately, it was later found that a similar workaround should be added for boxed slices, and this was applied in the 2024 edition."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Specifically, "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"IntoIterator"},{"type":"text","value":" is implemented for "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Box<[T]>"},{"type":"text","value":" on all editions, but specific calls to "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"into_iter()"},{"type":"text","value":" for boxed slices will defer to the slice implementation on editions before 2024:"}]},{"type":"link","href":"std/boxed/index.html#","title":"This example runs with edition 2021","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ⓘ"}]},{"type":"code_block","language":null,"code":"// Rust 2015, 2018, and 2021:\n\nlet boxed_slice: Box<[i32]> = vec![0; 3].into_boxed_slice();\n\n// This creates a slice iterator, producing references to each value.\nfor item in boxed_slice.into_iter().enumerate() {\n    let (i, x): (usize, &i32) = item;\n    println!(\"boxed_slice[{i}] = {x}\");\n}\n\n// The `boxed_slice_into_iter` lint suggests this change for future compatibility:\nfor item in boxed_slice.iter().enumerate() {\n    let (i, x): (usize, &i32) = item;\n    println!(\"boxed_slice[{i}] = {x}\");\n}\n\n// You can explicitly iterate a boxed slice by value using `IntoIterator::into_iter`\nfor item in IntoIterator::into_iter(boxed_slice).enumerate() {\n    let (i, x): (usize, i32) = item;\n    println!(\"boxed_slice[{i}] = {x}\");\n}"},{"type":"link","href":"https://play.rust-lang.org/?code=%23!%5Ballow(unused)%5D%0A//+Rust+2015,+2018,+and+2021:%0A%0A%23!%5Ballow(boxed_slice_into_iter)%5D%0A+//+override+our+%60deny(warnings)%60%0Afn+main()+%7B%0A++++let+boxed_slice:+Box%3C%5Bi32%5D%3E+=+vec!%5B0;+3%5D.into_boxed_slice();%0A++++%0A++++//+This+creates+a+slice+iterator,+producing+references+to+each+value.%0A++++for+item+in+boxed_slice.into_iter().enumerate()+%7B%0A++++++++let+(i,+x):+(usize,+%26i32)+=+item;%0A++++++++println!(%22boxed_slice%5B%7Bi%7D%5D+=+%7Bx%7D%22);%0A++++%7D%0A++++%0A++++//+The+%60boxed_slice_into_iter%60+lint+suggests+this+change+for+future+compatibility:%0A++++for+item+in+boxed_slice.iter().enumerate()+%7B%0A++++++++let+(i,+x):+(usize,+%26i32)+=+item;%0A++++++++println!(%22boxed_slice%5B%7Bi%7D%5D+=+%7Bx%7D%22);%0A++++%7D%0A++++%0A++++//+You+can+explicitly+iterate+a+boxed+slice+by+value+using+%60IntoIterator::into_iter%60%0A++++for+item+in+IntoIterator::into_iter(boxed_slice).enumerate()+%7B%0A++++++++let+(i,+x):+(usize,+i32)+=+item;%0A++++++++println!(%22boxed_slice%5B%7Bi%7D%5D+=+%7Bx%7D%22);%0A++++%7D%0A%7D&edition=2021","title":"Run code","children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Similar to the array implementation, this may be modified in the future to remove this override, and it’s best to avoid relying on this edition-dependent behavior if you wish to preserve compatibility with future versions of the compiler."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"structs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Structs"}]},{"type":"definition_list","items":[{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/boxed/struct.Box.html","title":"struct std::boxed::Box","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Box"}]}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"A pointer type that uniquely owns a heap allocation of type "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"T"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"link","href":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/boxed/struct.ThinBox.html","title":"struct std::boxed::ThinBox","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ThinBox"}]},{"type":"text","value":"Experimental"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"ThinBox."}]}]}]}}
